problem:
The data contradict the 2-lane reading of cells 34–36. With that reading, the frozen plant (c = 1, the one judged in §5) cannot hold B at the Pioneer Tr–Old Shakopee Rd pair while keeping the section downstream free.

Cells 34–36 carry the Old Shakopee Rd (OSR) station flow plus E15, which equals the measured T.H.101 station + X14 + X15. Reviewer read-only check on the cached 2023 Wednesdays (corridor lane_bins, 05:00–23:00):
- median daily maximum 15-min flow there: 5,132 veh/h (≈ 2,570 veh/h/lane on 2 lanes), against 4,004 veh/h at the OSR station (n = 51);
- on the 23 real-B-active days: 5,156 vs 3,932 veh/h, higher on 23/23 days;
- 15:00–19:00 means on B days: ≈ 4,300–4,600 veh/h, i.e. ≈ 2,200 veh/h/lane for 4 h, while T.H.101 runs at a median 69.5 mph during B episodes;
- class-B E15 ≈ 750 veh/h in the PM (median), up to ≈ 1,130.

The configuration records no lane added at E15 (entrance shift 5 = mainline 5) and lanes = 2 at rnd_144 and rnd_145.

Under v1, G3 has one per-lane capacity for cells 26–37, and c = 1 in §5:
- to keep cells 34–36 free, as they are in reality, the G3 capacity must be ≥ ≈ 2,580 veh/h/lane. Cell 33 then cannot break down at B's ≈ 1,965–2,000 veh/h/lane (P3 fails);
- with a capacity near B's, cells 34–36 (plus the δ_m merge of E15 in cell 34) jam every PM. The model bottleneck then sits downstream of the OSR station and is detected at pair OSR–T.H.101 (P2/P3 fail, and T.H.101-type congestion appears inside the model).

Neither remedy reaches this:
- the c grid bottom (0.80) is above the ≈ 0.76 needed (3,932 / 5,156), so most active days become 'not reproducible' in §4.7;
- F1 acts only on cells 31–33, after activation. A FAIL followed by an F1 FAIL and a route stop would follow from geometry, not plant realism.

No v1 check catches this before calibration, because E15's class-B balance closes the OSR → T.H.101 link and absorbs the inconsistency.

Possible causes:
- an unrecorded lane, e.g. an auxiliary lane from the Old Shakopee Rd entrance to the T.H.101 SB exit (X15 carries ≈ 1,300 veh/h in the PM). This is my inference [VERIFY imagery/IRIS];
- or inflated X14/X15/T.H.101 counts.

The plant that passes §5 is not the plant the gate uses:
- P1–P9 are judged at c = 1;
- the gate draws c_d from F̂ per day and seed;
- the stochastic plant's P3 and P6 are only reported.

Nothing ties F̂ to 1:
- it is estimated after the c = 1 calibration, with all else frozen;
- KM puts the remaining mass at 1.200;
- 'not reproducible' days (which are really left-censored at ≤ 0.80) are dropped;
- its median can land anywhere in [0.80, 1.20].

Breakdown occurrence is non-linear in c, so the gate's B-active rate per demand half can differ from the validated one in either direction. That includes more breakdowns than real, the false-GO direction that v1 explicitly guards against for the drop (§0.4, P5b).

Two decisions are left open:
- R3 defers 'whether c is re-centred' to the later gate protocol, which will be written after F̂ and its median are known. This is a forking path;
- the flag for '> 20 % of active calibration days not reproducible' has no pre-registered consequence.

The weave reading leaves the model with no way to put B's head where the data put it.

Why the head is forced into cell 33:
- E14 adds a lane (δ_m = 0) and cells 31–32 have λ = 3, so the weave has 1.5× the capacity of cell 33;
- c ≥ 0.80 and F1's Δ ≤ 0.15 scale all three B cells equally. Cells 31–32 never drop below 2.4 lane-equivalents, so they can never be the binding cell;
- the model's B is therefore the 3→2 exit-lane end at km 7.017 (no φ), i.e. cell 33 (7.017–7.492). That is the cell that also holds the OSR station (7.199). Effectively, 'B cells 31–33' means cell 33.

What the data show (reviewer check, 545 episode bins, all 52 Wednesdays): during B the OSR station is in discharge, median 56.7 mph at ≈ 20 veh/km/lane, while Pioneer Tr is at 26.1 mph.

What the model will likely do (my inference from the METANET structure, not run):
- the binding cell itself sits at or above ρ_cr, with speed at most about V(ρ_cr) = v_f·e^{−1/a}, ≈ 43–50 mph for v_f 117–129 km/h and a 1.9–2.5;
- the queue head is cell 33's downstream end (km 7.49), downstream of the station.

Consequences:
- the model's 'B downstream' station reads the bottleneck cell, not the discharge zone;
- the B-pair rule (Δv ≥ 20 mph), and with it P2, P3, P4 and the matching behind P5/P7/P9, then depend on the cell layout;
- when model OSR < 40 mph, the episode is detected at OSR–T.H.101 instead. P8 does not flag that pair, because it shares a station with B, but P2/P3 count it as a miss;
- F1 and c cannot move the head into the weave.

The merge variant (2 lanes, δ_m at E14) would put the head at the E14 cell, upstream of the OSR station, which matches the real signature.

The 10 % rule compares the passage–balance gap with the ramp's own volume, but the balance error scales with the mainline through flow (3,200–3,760 veh/h at E12/E13). A 1 % station error is 15–30 % of these ramps' PM flows (117–220 veh/h).

Reviewer read-only check on all 52 Wednesdays (daily and 15:00–19:00 sums, no ΔN term):
- E13 via S of Highwood (n = 51): Passage/balance 2.09 (day) and 1.17 (PM); Queue/balance 2.06 and 1.14. The Queue detector agrees with Passage, not with the balance;
- E12: Passage/balance 0.50 and 0.65; Queue/balance 0.55 and 0.76. No corroboration;
- E14: Passage/balance 1.50 and 1.235; Queue/balance 0.964 and 0.960. Genuine;
- E4: 1.20 and 1.12.

The rule would switch E12, E13 and E4, and §3.3.2 applies the switch even when the Queue detector does not corroborate. As a result:
- E13's daily inflow is roughly halved and E12's doubled. Both lie in B's queue area, and E12 feeds the φ cell 22;
- a mainline-count discrepancy (≈ 2 % of the link, which the §3.4 test would accept at its 3 % scale) is laundered into a ramp input;
- the link leaves the conservation check;
- its downstream station (S of Highwood; Pioneer Tr, B's upstream station) leaves S_q.

'− every measured exit flow up to and including X13' literally excludes the class-B exits. Rough PM station differences without the storage term (reviewer check) give:
- X6+X7 (T.H.62): ≈ 1,450 veh/h;
- X9 (W 78th St): ≈ 230 veh/h;
- X10 (I-494 EB): ≈ 510 veh/h.

Together that is ≈ 2,200 veh/h, about 60 % of B's PM throughput. Implemented literally:
- A_B exceeds the model Q_dis for most of the afternoon;
- excess is inflated and S2 = plateau / excess shrinks;
- the gated P9(2) (≥ 1.2) fails, or passes, for reasons unrelated to the plant.

The low- and high-drop variants are built with F1's switch, which raises two problems.

The parameter domain is undefined:
- Δ is 'negative if needed', which is outside F1's specified [0, 0.15];
- a negative Δ raises capacity after activation;
- the trigger and release behaviour with η is unspecified, so switching may chatter.

Validation days are used for fitting:
- Δ is set so that the plant's measured drop on the validation heavy matched days hits a target taken from those same days' real r (±0.5 pp);
- that is a plant parameter fitted on validation days, which contradicts §8.5 ('Validation days are never used for fitting').

'11 cells of 512.6 m' contradicts the §1.4 rule, which keeps the station break points. The rule gives:
- 3.751–6.038: 5 × 457.4 m (after the 171-m merge);
- 6.038–7.199: 2 × 580.5 m;
- 7.199–9.390: 4 × 547.75 m.

That is also 11 cells, but with different boundaries. The B cells (cell containing km 6.118 through the cell containing km 7.199) are then 2 cells, not the 3 that 512.6-m cells give (5.801–7.339).

A1's default calls the merge variant 'fully specified', yet code that follows the rule would fail the build assertion against this text.

A4 makes the gate plant stochastic (c_d ~ F̂) specifically to remove the false-GO bias of an over-predictable plant (review issue 11). Yet §5 gates only c = 1:
- stochastic P3, P6 and the Brier score are reported only;
- §0.2's 'not certified' list omits F̂;
- whether c is re-centred is deferred to the gate protocol (R3), i.e. chosen after F̂ and the P-results are known (a forking path).
No gated check protects against F̂ being too narrow (over-confident breakdown), the direction that favours the informed reference. P6(b)–(c) bound the model's demand gradient from below only. A step plant (no B in the lower half, B on every upper-half day) passes P6(a) with probability 0.41–0.74 at n_h = 8–12 with half shares 0.24/0.65, and always passes (b)–(c) (my binomial computation, stylised). Also, if P6's single-binomial tolerance were applied to the stochastic version, where the model count is random too, the false-FAIL of (a) is 13–21 % at n_h = 8–12, not ≈ 5 % (my computation).

F1 nests the base plant: Δ ∈ [0, 0.15], and unit test 7 says Δ = 0 reproduces the base. It is refitted from a cold start with 8 new seeds and a different popsize, and judged on the **same** validation days. An F1 PASS can therefore be a second calibration draw of the base structure, a re-roll of the optimiser rather than evidence for the two-capacity mechanism.

F1 is also triggered by any gated failure, including ones its structure cannot affect:
- N0;
- P1a;
- P3 (F1 acts only after breakdown, so it cannot change whether B breaks down);
- P6;
- P8 (off-B cells).
For these, only the refit can change the verdict. This gives two looks at one validation set with no label, and the conjunction's false-PASS rate for a bad plant roughly doubles (inference).

proposed_change:
Add an explicit, non-iterated order to §3:
1. lane-day validity (both layers);
2. E2, E3;
3. §3.4 classification on the calibration days passing 1–2, with the denominator = days on which the link is closed;
4. lane screen and c_s on the calibration days passing 1–3 and the E1 data checks other than the speed-valid share;
5. complete E1 with the frozen retained lanes;
6. E4, with its reference over the calibration days passing 1–5.

Each step is computed once. State that Q_A excludes stations dropped by §3.2.4.

problem:
P-T is the only check of the plant on the years the gate uses, but:
- it has no minimum n and no INCONCLUSIVE route;
- it gates only N0, P1a and P3;
- it does not say that E1–E4 apply.
P1a is probably weakly discriminating in replay (an inference): station flows are close to identities of the replayed inputs on links that pass the 5 % conservation rule, and v1 itself calls P1b non-discriminating. P-T therefore reduces mostly to P3, which at an unspecified n (the gate draft mentions 14 days) is noisy. Nothing checks that the B mechanism (onset timing, discharge, drop) transfers, although metering strategies or detectors may have changed in 2024.

